[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mocking Constructors in Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Traditionally, mocking object creation inside a method under test required refactoring to use Dependency Injection or relying on bytecode manipulation frameworks like PowerMock. Starting with Mockito 3.5.0, Mockito introduced native support for constructor mocking via the Mockito.mockConstruction() API. This feature allows developers to intercept object instantiations made with the 'new' keyword and return configured mock instances instead."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Dependencies"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Constructor mocking requires Mockito version 3.5.0 or higher. For Mockito 3.x, the 'mockito-inline' dependency is required. In Mockito 5.x and above, constructor mocking capabilities are integrated directly into 'mockito-core'."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<dependency>
    <groupId>org.mockito</groupId>
    <artifactId>mockito-core</artifactId>
    <version>5.11.0</version>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Constructor Mocking with try-with-resources"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Constructor mocks are scoped to the current thread and must be explicitly closed to avoid affecting other tests. Using Java's try-with-resources statement ensures that the construction mocking scope is properly cleaned up when the execution finishes."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"@Test
void testBasicConstructorMocking() {
    try (MockedConstruction<PaymentProcessor> mocked = Mockito.mockConstruction(PaymentProcessor.class)) {
        OrderService service = new OrderService();
        service.processOrder("ORDER_123");

        // Assert that exactly one instance was constructed
        assertEquals(1, mocked.constructed().size());

        // Retrieve the created mock
        PaymentProcessor constructedMock = mocked.constructed().get(0);
        verify(constructedMock).charge("ORDER_123");
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Mock Behavior on Instantiation"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can supply a mock initializer callback as a second parameter to mockConstruction(). This allows you to stub method calls or define default return values whenever a new instance of the target class is created."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@Test
void testCustomConstructorBehavior() {
    try (MockedConstruction<PaymentProcessor> mocked = Mockito.mockConstruction(
            PaymentProcessor.class,
            (mock, context) -> {
                // Stub methods on every newly created mock instance
                when(mock.charge(anyString())).thenReturn(true);
            })) {

        OrderService service = new OrderService();
        boolean result = service.processOrder("ORDER_123");

        assertTrue(result);
        PaymentProcessor constructedMock = mocked.constructed().get(0);
        verify(constructedMock).charge("ORDER_123");
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Limitations"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always declare MockedConstruction inside a try-with-resources block to prevent thread-leakage of static and constructor mocks."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Constructor mocking works per-thread. Objects instantiated in asynchronous or parallel threads will not be automatically mocked unless configured properly."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Treat constructor mocking as a tool for legacy codebases. For new code, prefer Dependency Injection (DI) to pass dependencies explicitly, improving testability and design."}]
